Embedded React: unique animation handles, and an end for every animation.

Modified from Embedded React (Apache-2.0, Copyright 2026 Cory Lamming) at the
revision pinned in dependencies.lock.json, after the patches listed before this
one there. Changed files: engine/animation/animation.c,
bridges/quickjs/native_ui_bridge.c, engine/tests/animation/test_animation.c and
bridges/quickjs/js/test/runtime (new anim-dropped.runtime.test.js). Not yet
proposed upstream.

Animated values, animations and animation groups share one 16-bit handle
counter, and every animation started takes a handle. A long session wraps it
(Pandora's grid takes about 18 per held-key step, so a few minutes of
scrolling), after which the counter gave out handles still held by live
values. Lookups by handle find the first slot that matches, so the new value
bound and drove the older value's node properties, and destroying it freed the
older value while its own slot stayed in use. Once the counter has wrapped,
alloc_handle now skips handles a live value, animation or group holds; before
the first wrap nothing changes.

An animation the engine cannot run (its value is gone, or every animation slot
is busy) returned without calling on_complete, so the bridge's completion slot
for its JS callback stayed taken; once the bridge's pool was full no animation
told JS it had ended. animValueAnimate now ends such an animation at once with
finished false and frees the slot.

test_animation creates and destroys 70,000 values beside one that lives
throughout and checks that its handle is not given again and that it alone
drives its property. anim-dropped animates a destroyed value 300 times and
checks that each ends and that a live animation still reports its end.

